docs: correct a comment that claimed login had no brute-force protection

#1250 shipped a comment on UserController::login saying brute-force
protection was missing and left as follow-up. That was WRONG, and a wrong
comment about a security control is worse than no comment: it tells the next
reader to go build something that already exists, or to treat a protected
endpoint as exposed.

login() has had complete brute-force protection all along, implemented
app-locally rather than through NC's IThrottler:

  SecurityService::checkLoginRateLimit()  gates entry, with lockout + delay
  recordFailedLoginAttempt()              on both failure paths
  recordSuccessfulLogin()                 clears on success

That is exactly the two-halves shape ADR-082 asks for -- and it is keyed on
USERNAME + IP, finer-grained than the framework throttler's IP-only key.
Adding #[BruteForceProtection] on top would be a second, coarser counter for
the same event.

I wrote the original note from the ABSENCE of an IThrottler call, which is
the same mistake this sweep already made once: pass 2 of the fleet count
declared launchpad's protection "decorative" because the counter lived in a
service instead of the controller. Twice now, looking for one specific
mechanism and concluding "unprotected" when a different one was doing the
job.

The AnonRateLimit stays: the volume ceiling is genuinely what app-local logic
did not cover.
